this is the aftermath of peak wildfire  season in greece  in this picturesque town about 30  kilometers north of athens  there's an eerie quiet as people take  stock of what has been lost  hectares of land burned  and homes destroyed  24 year old ariavni lived in this house  her entire life with her family  on july 20th within the span of an hour  everything her family had was gone  so this was the living room  and here was a it was our  the table where we used to eat  ariuvani's family home is the only house  in this area that was destroyed  and there was a studio there with drums  and guitars this house is one of the  many homes in the country that is not  insured  according to the hellenic association of  insurance companies  only 15 percent of homes in greece have  insurance  no why not  because we didn't think that it would  happen to us  but it did so it can happen to anyone  that's not the only reason most people  here don't insure their homes  maria polansa owns one of the oldest and  biggest insurance agencies in athens  according to her agency's figures since  the wildfires started destroying homes  in the past two years  there has only been about a two to three  percent increase in insurance contracts  i think the main reason is that they  haven't understood how valuable their  property is only when they lose it they  understand and also they have not  realized how inexpensive it is to have  home insurance if they comprehend that  home insurance cost roughly one euro per  square meter per year i believe that  most greek people would consider  insuring their homes  one of the other main issues here is the  government has not been able to mandate  the purchase of home insurance  most properties in greece are passed on  through family inheritance so most  people don't have mortgages that means  they are not legally forced to purchase  home insurance  the government has tried to force  homeowners to buy insurance only in  certain parts of the country a move  which outraged the insurance companies  and forced it to abandon the idea but it  does provide some financial assistance  to those who have lost their homes to  natural disasters  for now many greeks remain uninsured and  face financial ruin with every fire  season  jabari al jazeera athens
 

